(3090726, 428367, 18197, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club: I have been assigned your photo to critique and here are my thoughts:<br/> <br/>Personal Reaction: Looking at your portfolio, I recognize this is your first challenge. Congratulations on your entry and the courage it took to enter. I remember very well my first time was scary.<br/> <br/>Composition: Generally, a centered and symmetrical composition can be improved by placing the subject off center. In this case, the green shade is centered but the bulb is off center a bit. The off-centered bulb helps the composition. You might have a look at this thread for more information: <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=168935&amp;highlight=composition%20rule%20of%20thirds" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=168935&amp;highlight=composition%20rule%20of%20thirds</a> <br/> <br/>Technicals: The focus is fine. The bulb a bit over exposed. I like the way the background disappears. I like the grainy texture but many DPC voters do not.<br/> <br/>Conclusions: In the final analysis, the subject doesnt seem that relevant to the challenge theme. I agree you have shown your subject from a certain perspective. But more closely relating the photo to the challenge theme will raise your scores.<br/> <br/>As always, this is my personal opinion. Feel free to PM me if youd like further dialog.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: I have been assigned your photo to critique and here are my thoughts: Personal Reaction: Looking at your portfolio, I recognize this is your first challenge. Congratulations on your entry and the courage it took to enter. I remember very well my first time was scary. Composition: Generally, a centered and symmetrical composition can be improved by placing the subject off center. In this case, the green shade is centered but the bulb is off center a bit. The off-centered bulb helps the composition. You might have a look at this thread for more information: //www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&FORUM_THREAD_ID=168935&highlight=composition%20rule%20of%20thirds  Technicals: The focus is fine. The bulb a bit over exposed. I like the way the background disappears. I like the grainy texture but many DPC voters do not. Conclusions: In the final analysis, the subject doesnt seem that relevant to the challenge theme. I agree you have shown your subject from a certain perspective. But more closely relating the photo to the challenge theme will raise your scores. As always, this is my personal opinion. Feel free to PM me if youd like further dialog.', '2006-12-05 23:36:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(3090752, 429567, 18197, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club: I have been assigned your photo to critique and here are my thoughts:<br/> <br/>Personal Reaction: I am a mathematician by training, and a computer science engineer by vocation. I really liked your photo and it appealed to my sense of symmetry and mathematical precision. Well done.<br/> <br/>Composition: Normally I am not an advocate for a centered composition but here it works well.<br/> <br/>Technicals: Focus is perfect, as is lighting, DoF and so on. Technically, I can suggest no improvements. I am impressed with the clarity of the image given its very long exposure. <br/> <br/>Conclusions: A pleasing abstract image, mesmerizing in its simplicity. It resonated well with those who commented, but not quite as well with the general population at DPC. Possibly a stretch for the general notion of perspective? <br/> <br/>As always, this is my personal opinion. Feel free to PM me if youd like further dialog.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: I have been assigned your photo to critique and here are my thoughts: Personal Reaction: I am a mathematician by training, and a computer science engineer by vocation. I really liked your photo and it appealed to my sense of symmetry and mathematical precision. Well done. Composition: Normally I am not an advocate for a centered composition but here it works well. Technicals: Focus is perfect, as is lighting, DoF and so on. Technically, I can suggest no improvements. I am impressed with the clarity of the image given its very long exposure.  Conclusions: A pleasing abstract image, mesmerizing in its simplicity. It resonated well with those who commented, but not quite as well with the general population at DPC. Possibly a stretch for the general notion of perspective?  As always, this is my personal opinion. Feel free to PM me if youd like further dialog.', '2006-12-05 23:48:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(3090755, 429567, 19708, '<td valign="top">Recognized this instantly.<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=86308" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/231/thumb/86308.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/231/thumb/86308.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/231/thumb/86308.jpg"/></a> <br/>:))<br/> <br/></td>', 'Recognized this instantly. :)) ', '2006-12-05 23:51:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(3090781, 429979, 18197, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club: I have been assigned your photo to critique and here are my thoughts:<br/> <br/>Personal Reaction: Cool. I have attempted many many photos of the London Eye. It is hard to take something new. You have given us a new and unique perspective on this London landmark. I like your creative take on it.<br/> <br/>Composition: There are many things to like about the composition. The triangle formed by legs and ground is terrific! The wheel in the background is well placed in the frame. Having the shoes break the edges of the image was inspired.<br/> <br/>Technicals: Focus is fine. DoF is good. But the image is a bit flat. Try increasing the contrast of the image just a bit and possibly sharpening a bit more. You can increase contrast in Photoshop with Image&gt;Adjustment&gt;Brightness/Contrast  or use a curves layer to increase mid-tone contrast. To sharpen you might try an Unsharp Mask filter. Other editing programs will have similar capabilities.<br/> <br/>Conclusions: You have only entered a couple of challenges but I like what I see so far. Keep it up!<br/> <br/>As always, this is my personal opinion. Feel free to PM me if youd like further dialog.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: I have been assigned your photo to critique and here are my thoughts: Personal Reaction: Cool. I have attempted many many photos of the London Eye. It is hard to take something new. You have given us a new and unique perspective on this London landmark. I like your creative take on it. Composition: There are many things to like about the composition. The triangle formed by legs and ground is terrific! The wheel in the background is well placed in the frame. Having the shoes break the edges of the image was inspired. Technicals: Focus is fine. DoF is good. But the image is a bit flat. Try increasing the contrast of the image just a bit and possibly sharpening a bit more. You can increase contrast in Photoshop with Image>Adjustment>Brightness/Contrast  or use a curves layer to increase mid-tone contrast. To sharpen you might try an Unsharp Mask filter. Other editing programs will have similar capabilities. Conclusions: You have only entered a couple of challenges but I like what I see so far. Keep it up! As always, this is my personal opinion. Feel free to PM me if youd like further dialog.', '2006-12-06 00:05:57.000000', NULL, 0),
